/* Nelson Textbook data chunk 04/07; generated only from absi/Nelson.zip. */
window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_NELSON_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-035","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A highly educated young couple has their first baby with Down syndrome; they are asking you about his development. Of the following, the MosT appropriate answer is","o":["programs of stimulation will achieve eventual normal development","motor development is the mostly affected domain with fair cognitive delay","developmental delay will be in the initial first few years, but it will normalize later on","developmental delay is universal, all domains of development almost equally affected","developmental delay is universal, social development is relatively spared but he will have problems with expressive language"],"k":4,"ex":"All children with Down syndrome have developmental delay, but not equally across domains. Social skills and adaptive behavior are relatively preserved, while expressive language (and speech) is markedly impaired out of proportion to comprehension.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Stimulation programs achieve normal development: false.\n\n- **B.** Motor development mostly affected with fair cognition: false (cognition and language are impaired).\n- **C.** Delay only in early years then normalizes: false.\n- **D.** All domains equally affected: false; social skills are relatively spared.\n- **E.** Correct (source answer): universal delay, relatively spared social development, but problems with expressive language.\n\nNelson explanation:\n35.(E). Developmental delay is universal. Cognitive impairment does not uniformly\naffect all areas of development. Social development is relatively spared, but children\n125\n[Source page 136]\nwith Down syndrome have considerable difficulty using expressive language.\nUnderstanding these individual developmental strengths will maximize the educational\nprocess for children with Down syndrome. Persons with Down syndrome often benefit\nfrom programs aimed at stimulation, development, and education. These programs are\nmost effective in addressing social skills that often appear advanced for the intellectual\ndelay.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 130 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":35,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-038","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Quad screen is the four maternal serum tests that should be done for all pregnant women in second trimester as a screening for Down syndrome, which includes the following tests EXCEPT","o":["free β-human chorionic gonadotropin",".unconjugated estriol","androsterone","inhibin","α-fetoprotein"],"k":2,"ex":"The quadruple (quad) screen at 15–20 weeks measures maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), unconjugated estriol (uE3), human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) and inhibin A. Down syndrome pattern: low AFP, low estriol, high hCG, high inhibin A.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Free beta-hCG: part of the quad screen (raised in Down syndrome).\n\n- **B.** Unconjugated estriol: part of the screen (low in Down syndrome) (OCR: letter B lost, stray '.').\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): androsterone – not part of the quad test (key).\n- **D.** Inhibin A: part of the screen (raised in Down syndrome).\n- **E.** Alpha-fetoprotein: part of the screen (low in Down syndrome).\n\nNelson explanation:\n38.(C). Androsterone is generally considered to be an inactive metabolite of\ntestosterone, and it is not related to quad test.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 131 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":38,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-041","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Robertsonian translocations involve 2 acrocentric chromosomes that fuse near the centromeric region with a subsequent loss of the short arms. It can occur in the following chromosomes ExCEPT","o":["13","15","17","21","22"],"k":2,"ex":"Robertsonian translocation is fusion of two acrocentric chromosomes at the centromere with loss of the short arms. The acrocentric human chromosomes are 13, 14, 15, 21 and 22; chromosome 17 is not acrocentric.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Chromosome 13: acrocentric (t(13;14) is the commonest).\n\n- **B.** Chromosome 15: acrocentric.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): chromosome 17 is not acrocentric (key also adds chromosome 14).\n- **D.** Chromosome 21: acrocentric (translocation Down syndrome).\n- **E.** Chromosome 22: acrocentric.\n\nNelson explanation:\n41.(C). It includes also chromosome number 14.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 131 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":41,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-042","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"One of the common chromosomal deletions which can lead to retinoblastoma is","o":["4p-","5p-","9p-","13q-","18p- 121 [Continuation on source page 132]"],"k":3,"ex":"Retinoblastoma results from loss of both RB1 tumor-suppressor alleles; RB1 lies at 13q14, so deletion of 13q is associated with retinoblastoma (also with intellectual disability and dysmorphism). Other deletions: 4p- Wolf-Hirschhorn, 5p- cri-du-chat, 9p- trigonocephaly, 18p- holoprosencephaly features.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** 4p-: Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome.\n\n- **B.** 5p-: cri-du-chat syndrome.\n- **C.** 9p-: 9p deletion syndrome (trigonocephaly).\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): 13q- (RB1 gene, retinoblastoma).\n- **E.** 18p-: 18p deletion syndrome.\n\nNelson explanation:\n42.(D).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source answer key gives the letter only.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 131 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":42,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-043","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are examples of microdeletion syndromes EXCEPT","o":["Williams","Prader-Willi","Angelman","DiGeorge","Wolf-Hirschhorn"],"k":4,"ex":"Microdeletion (contiguous gene) syndromes involve small deletions detectable by FISH or microarray: Williams (7q11.23), Prader-Willi and Angelman (15q11-q13), DiGeorge (22q11.2), Alagille (20p12). Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome (4p16.3) is a terminal deletion syndrome of the short arm of chromosome 4; the key classes it as a chromosomal deletion syndrome rather than a microdeletion.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Williams: 7q11.23 microdeletion.\n\n- **B.** Prader-Willi: paternal 15q11-q13 microdeletion.\n- **C.** Angelman: maternal 15q11-q13 microdeletion.\n- **D.** DiGeorge: 22q11.2 microdeletion.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT, per key): Wolf-Hirschhorn (4p-) is a chromosomal deletion syndrome.\n\nNelson explanation:\n43.(E). Microdeletions involve loss of small chromosome regions so that the affected\nindividuals can have a distinctive phenotype depending on the number of genes\ninvolved. All the mentioned syndromes are common microdeletions and the board\ncandidate should be familial with their phenotypic descriptions and clinical\nmanifestations. Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome is chromosomal deletion syndrome.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): some sources also list Wolf-Hirschhorn among deletion syndromes (terminal 4p16.3 deletions, often submicroscopic), so the 'EXCEPT' relies on Nelson's classification. Source answer preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 132 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":43,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-044","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"An 18-month-old female child had been diagnosed as Turner syndrome (45, XO), FISH analysis shows Y chromosome mosaicism. Of the following, the MosT appropriate next step is to","o":["refer to endocrinologist","reassure as the course will be milder","advise for feminizing hormones","arrange for laparoscopic removal of gonads","rear the child as male gender"],"k":3,"ex":"Phenotypic females with Turner syndrome who carry Y-chromosome material (45,X/46,XY mosaicism) have a risk of gonadoblastoma, which the key quotes as 15–30%. FISH for Y sequences is recommended in 45,X patients; if Y material is found, prophylactic laparoscopic gonadectomy is advised.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Refer to an endocrinologist: needed for care, but not the definitive next step here.\n\n- **B.** Reassure that the course will be milder: wrong; malignancy risk exists.\n- **C.** Advise feminizing hormones: estrogen at puberty is part of treatment but not the immediate next step.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): laparoscopic gonadectomy to prevent gonadoblastoma.\n- **E.** Rear as male: wrong; she is a phenotypic female.\n\nNelson explanation:\n44.(D). Phenotypic females with 45, X/46, XY mosaicism have a 15-30% risk of\ndeveloping gonadoblastoma. The American Academy of Pediatrics has recommended\nthe use of FISH analysis to look for Y-chromosome mosaicism in all 45, X patients. If Y\nchromosome material is identified, laparoscopic gonadectomy is recommended.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 132 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":44,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-045","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are recognized features of Turner syndrome ExcEPT","o":["female phenotype","male phenotype","hypothyroidism","type I diabetes mellitus","gonadal dysgenesis"],"k":3,"ex":"Turner syndrome (45,X) is a female phenotype with gonadal dysgenesis, short stature, and associations with hypothyroidism (Hashimoto), glucose intolerance/insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes, celiac disease, coarctation and renal anomalies. The key calls type 1 diabetes the exception and says that a male phenotype can occur in mosaic (45,X/46,XY) cases.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Female phenotype: the usual phenotype.\n\n- **B.** Male phenotype: possible with 45,X/46,XY mosaicism per the key (strictly mixed gonadal dysgenesis).\n- **C.** Hypothyroidism: recognized (autoimmune).\n- **D.** Keyed answer (source answer D): type 1 diabetes mellitus; the key says type 2/insulin resistance is the true association.\n- **E.** Gonadal dysgenesis: the characteristic ovarian failure.\n\nNelson explanation:\n45.(D). Male phenotype can occur with mosaic type of Turner syndrome, and type lI\ninsulin resistance is the true answer.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required (moderate): SHETS lists 'DM' without type and type 1 diabetes is also associated with Turner syndrome; option B is also an unusual statement. Recommend review. Source answer D preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 132 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":45,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GENETICSCO-046","spec":"genetics","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"All the following are characteristic features of fragile X chromosome of a 3-year-old male child EXCEPT","o":["intellectual disability","autistic behavior","macroorchidism","hyperextensible finger joints","characteristic facial features 122"],"k":2,"ex":"Fragile X syndrome (FMR1 CGG repeat expansion, X-linked) causes moderate intellectual disability, autistic-like behavior, hyperactivity, and connective-tissue features (joint hyperextensibility, mitral valve prolapse, flat feet). Macroorchidism is postpubertal and typically not evident in a 3-year-old; facial features (long face, large ears, prominent jaw) are subtle in early childhood and become more obvious with age.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Intellectual disability: characteristic.\n\n- **B.** Autistic behavior: characteristic.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): macroorchidism is not evident until puberty (key).\n- **D.** Hyperextensible finger joints: characteristic connective-tissue feature.\n- **E.** Characteristic facial features: present but more obvious with age.\n\nNelson explanation:\n46.(C). The macroorchidism may not be evident until puberty. The facial features,\nwhich include a long face, large ears, and a prominent square jaw, become more\nobvious with age.\n126","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Human Genetics · question page 132 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Genetics & Dermatology","nlNumber":46,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-001","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"All the following are causes oropharyngeal dysphagia ExCEPT","o":["cerebral palsy","esophagitis","brain tumors","cerebrovascular accidents","hyperthyroidism"],"k":1,"ex":"Esophagitis causes esophageal dysphagia (food sticking after the swallow has started, often painful), not oropharyngeal dysphagia.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nDysphagia is classified by site. Oropharyngeal dysphagia is difficulty initiating the swallow and coordinating the oral and pharyngeal phases; it is typically neuromuscular (cerebral palsy, myopathy, myasthenia gravis, brainstem lesions) and shows as coughing or choking at the start of the swallow. Esophageal dysphagia comes from obstruction or dysmotility of the esophageal body: esophagitis, strictures (including corrosive), achalasia, tumor, TEF, vascular ring, extrinsic compression.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Cerebral palsy: classic neuromuscular cause of poor oral-pharyngeal coordination.\n\n- C. Brain tumors: central or brainstem involvement impairs the swallowing mechanism.\n- D. Cerebrovascular accidents: bulbar or pseudobulbar involvement impairs swallowing.\n- E. Hyperthyroidism: not addressed in the curriculum and its mechanism is not given in the supplied files; the source key treats it as a non-esophageal cause, so it is not the EXCEPT option.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.292 (book p.282) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":1,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-002","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"Allthe following are common causes of emesis in childhood ExCEPT","o":["gastroenteritis","toxic ingestion","pertussis syndrome","peptic ulcer","otitis media"],"k":3,"ex":"Peptic ulcer is an uncommon cause of vomiting in childhood.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nMost vomiting in infants and children is due to infections (gastroenteritis, otitis media and other extra-GI infections, pertussis with post-tussive vomiting), feeding problems, and drugs, toxins or food poisoning. Peptic ulcer belongs to the GIT causes but is rare in children; it is suspected when epigastric pain wakes the child at night, with hematemesis, or with a first-degree relative with ulcer disease.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Gastroenteritis: the commonest cause of acute vomiting.\n\n- B. Toxic ingestion: drugs, poisons and food poisoning cause acute emesis.\n- C. Pertussis syndrome: vomiting at the end of paroxysmal coughing is a listed red flag.\n- E. Otitis media: extra-GI infection commonly presenting with vomiting and fever.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.292 (book p.282) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":2,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-003","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Secretory diarrhea can be caused by","o":["neuroblastoma","laxative abuse","lactase deficiency","irritable bowel syndrome","thyrotoxicosis"],"k":0,"ex":"Neuroblastoma can produce a secretory diarrhea (tumour-derived secretagogues cause active intestinal secretion).","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nSecretory diarrhea results from increased active secretion and decreased absorption of electrolytes and water (toxin-induced cAMP rise in cholera and ETEC, or hormone-secreting tumours such as neuroblastoma). Features: large volume (>200 mL/day), not related to food, continues during fasting, normal stool osmolarity and pH, no reducing substances. Osmotic diarrhea is the opposite pattern (stops with fasting, acidic stool, positive reducing substances). A third mechanism is increased motility.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. Laxative abuse: per the source it is an osmotic cause.\n\n- C. Lactase deficiency: unabsorbed lactose gives osmotic diarrhea.\n- D. Irritable bowel syndrome: increased bowel motility.\n- E. Thyrotoxicosis: increased bowel motility.\n\nHigh-yield:\nSecretory diarrhea persists with fasting; osmotic diarrhea stops with fasting.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.292 (book p.282) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":3,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-004","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"All the following metabolic disorders can cause constipation ExCEPT","o":["hypercalcemia","hyperkalemia","hypothyroidism","diabetes mellitus","diabetes insipidus"],"k":1,"ex":"Hypokalemia (not hyperkalemia) is the electrolyte disturbance that causes constipation.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nMetabolic and endocrine disorders slow gut transit or dry the stool. Hypokalemia reduces smooth-muscle contractility (ileus/constipation). Hypercalcemia reduces gut motility. Hypothyroidism slows transit. Polyuria with water loss (diabetes mellitus, diabetes insipidus) leaves hard stool. Hyperkalemia is not a cause, so it is the EXCEPT option.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Hypercalcemia: listed metabolic cause of constipation.\n\n- C. Hypothyroidism: listed endocrine cause of constipation.\n- D. Diabetes mellitus: accepted by the source as a cause (not listed in the curriculum's constipation slide).\n- E. Diabetes insipidus: accepted by the source as a cause (not listed in the curriculum's constipation slide).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.292 (book p.282) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":4,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-005","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"All the following suggest a potentially serious organic etiology of abdominal pain EXCEPT","o":["age >5 yr","fever","flank pain","awakening from sleep","referred pain to shoulder"],"k":0,"ex":"Age >5 years does not suggest organic disease; functional abdominal pain is typical at 5-12 years, and the source notes that younger age (<5 years) raises the suspicion of an organic cause.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nChronic or recurrent abdominal pain is functional in about 90% of school-age children (typically 5-12 years, periumbilical, normal growth, no alarm signs). Red flags for organic disease include fever, weight loss, persistent pain away from the umbilicus (RUQ/RLQ), pain that wakes the child, GI bleeding, genitourinary symptoms and dysphagia.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. Fever is a constitutional red flag.\n\n- C. Flank pain points toward a urinary tract cause (the curriculum lists dysuria and GU symptoms as red flags).\n- D. Awakening from sleep is a listed red flag (night-time waking).\n- E. Referred pain to the shoulder suggests irritation of the diaphragm or an upper-abdominal organ; not in the curriculum but consistent with the source key.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.292 (book p.282) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":5,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-007","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Delayed eruption of the primary teeth can be due to the following EXCEPT","o":["familial","hypopituitarism","hyperthyroidism","cleidocranial dysplasia","trisomy 21"],"k":2,"ex":"Hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism, delays tooth eruption.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nDelayed teething means no primary tooth by about 13 months. Causes: familial/idiopathic, rickets, Down syndrome, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism, achondroplasia, osteogenesis imperfecta, and cleidocranial dysplasia.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Familial (idiopathic) delay is a common benign cause.\n\n- B. Hypopituitarism: listed cause.\n- D. Cleidocranial dysplasia: recognised cause per the source (not in the curriculum list).\n- E. Trisomy 21: listed cause.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.293 (book p.283) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":7,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-010","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Aphthous-like lesions may be associated with the following conditions ExCEPT","o":["inflammatory bowel disease","Behcet disease","gluten-sensitive enteropathy","SWEET syndrome","herpetic gingivostomatitis"],"k":4,"ex":"Herpetic gingivostomatitis is a separate infectious (HSV-1) ulcerative disease, not an aphthous-like lesion.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nAphthous ulcers are recurrent, well-circumscribed painful ulcers with a white base and red halo; they can accompany IBD, Behcet disease, celiac disease, SLE, cyclic neutropenia. Herpetic gingivostomatitis (HSV-1, preschool children) presents with fever, irritability, gingivitis and vesicles that ulcerate, treated supportively.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. IBD: listed association.\n\n- B. Behcet disease: listed association.\n- C. Gluten-sensitive enteropathy (celiac): listed association.\n- D. Sweet syndrome: recognised association per the source (not in the curriculum).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.293 (book p.283) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":10,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-013","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"One of the following cause osmotic diarrhea","o":["toxigenic Escherichiacoli","carcinoid","neuroblastoma","glucose-galactose malabsorption","congenital chloride diarrhea"],"k":3,"ex":"Glucose-galactose malabsorption leaves unabsorbed monosaccharides in the lumen, producing osmotic diarrhea.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nOsmotic diarrhea is due to non-absorbable solutes (lactulose, disaccharide or monosaccharide malabsorption): stool volume <200 mL/day, stops with fasting, acidic stool, positive reducing substances, high osmolar gap.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Toxigenic E. coli (ETEC): toxin raises cAMP, secretory diarrhea.\n\n- B. Carcinoid: hormone-mediated secretory diarrhea (not in the curriculum).\n- C. Neuroblastoma: secretory diarrhea.\n- E. Congenital chloride diarrhea: defective ion transport, secretory (listed in the curriculum only as a cause of chronic diarrhea).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.294 (book p.284) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":13,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-014","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"Acute diarrhea in infancy is commonly caused by","o":["primary disaccharidase deficiency","overfeeding","Hirschsprung toxic colitis","adrenogenital syndrome","neonatal opiate withdrawal"],"k":1,"ex":"Overfeeding (feeding errors) is a common non-infectious cause of acute diarrhea in infants.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nAcute diarrhea in infancy is mostly infectious, but feeding errors (too much, too little, wrong food) and drugs are the usual non-infectious causes. The other options are rare causes.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Primary disaccharidase deficiency: rare.\n\n- C. Hirschsprung toxic colitis: rare, enterocolitis in aganglionosis.\n- D. Adrenogenital syndrome: rare cause.\n- E. Neonatal opiate withdrawal: rare cause.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.294 (book p.284) | Answer PDF p.311 (book p.301)","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":14,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-017","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Pierre Robin syndrome consists of the following EXCEPT","o":["micrognathia","high arched palate","cleft palate","small tongue","foreshortened floor of the mouth"],"k":3,"ex":"In Pierre Robin sequence the tongue is not small; it is displaced backwards (glossoptosis) because of the small mandible.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nPierre Robin sequence = micrognathia + glossoptosis + (U-shaped) cleft palate. The tongue is usually of normal size but the floor of the mouth is foreshortened, so the tongue falls back and can obstruct the airway, especially on inspiration.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Micrognathia: the primary defect.\n\n- B. High arched palate: accepted as a feature by the source (not specifically in the curriculum).\n- C. Cleft palate: part of the triad.\n- E. Foreshortened floor of the mouth: described by the source.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.294-295 (book p.284-285) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":17,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-019","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Contrast (usually barium) radiographic study of the esophagus and upper gastrointestinal tract has poor sensitivity and specificity in the diagnosis of","o":["achalasia","esophageal strictures","GERD","hiatal hernia","gastric outlet obstruction"],"k":2,"ex":"A barium study shows anatomy, not reflux; it has poor sensitivity and specificity for GERD.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nContrast studies are used to detect anatomical problems: achalasia (bird beak), strictures, hiatal hernia, gastric outlet or intestinal obstruction. The best test for GERD is 24-hour esophageal pH monitoring (pH <4 for much of the time); endoscopy diagnoses esophagitis; scintigraphy shows gastric emptying/aspiration.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Achalasia: barium swallow gives the bird-beak sign.\n\n- B. Esophageal strictures: well shown by contrast.\n- D. Hiatal hernia: anatomical, shown by contrast.\n- E. Gastric outlet obstruction: delineated by upper GI contrast.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.295 (book p.285) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":19,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-020","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is the most common esophageal disorder in children of all ages. Of the following, the MOsT common symptom in infants is","o":["excessive crying","regurgitation","failure to thrive","abnormal posturing","sleeping disturbances"],"k":1,"ex":"Regurgitation, especially after feeding, is the most frequent GERD symptom in infants.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nInfant reflux is mostly regurgitation (non-forceful, non-bilious, post-prandial). The other items occur in complicated reflux (esophagitis, failure to thrive, Sandifer-type posturing).\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Excessive crying: sign of esophagitis, less common.\n\n- C. Failure to thrive: complication of severe reflux.\n- D. Abnormal posturing: Sandifer syndrome, uncommon.\n- E. Sleeping disturbances: less common.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.295 (book p.285) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":20,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-021","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"The following procedures satisfactorily improve gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) symptoms in infants EXCEPT","o":["modified feeding volumes","hydrolyzed infant formulas","prone position, when the infant is awake and observed","avoidance of smoke exposure","seated position"],"k":4,"ex":"Per the source, a seated position worsens infant reflux and should be avoided; the other measures help.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nUncomplicated infant reflux is managed conservatively: small frequent feeds with appropriate volume, thickened or anti-reflux formula, a trial of hydrolyzed formula if cow's milk protein allergy is suspected, avoidance of tobacco smoke, and prone positioning only while awake and observed (never for sleep, because of SIDS risk).\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Modified feeding volumes: help.\n\n- B. Hydrolyzed formula: helps when cow's milk protein allergy is the cause.\n- C. Prone position when awake and observed: reduces reflux.\n- D. Avoiding smoke exposure: reduces reflux symptoms.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.295 (book p.285) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":21,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-025","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Meckel diverticulum has been conveniently referred to by the \"rule of 2s,\" which explains the classic presentation of this congenital anomaly. One of the following is FALSE in this rule","o":["found in approximately 2% of the general population","usually located 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve","approximately 2 inches in length","contain 2 types of ectopic tissue (pancreatic or gastric)","found twice as commonly in males"],"k":4,"ex":"Per the source key, the false statement is the one about sex distribution (the source states Meckel diverticulum is twice as common in females).","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nMeckel diverticulum is a persistent vitello-intestinal duct remnant. Rule of 2s: about 2% of the population, about 2 inches long, within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve, 2 types of ectopic tissue (gastric and pancreatic), and presents with complications mostly in the first years of life.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. True: about 2% of the population.\n\n- B. True: located about 2 feet from the ileocecal valve.\n- C. True: about 2 inches in length.\n- D. True: gastric and pancreatic ectopic tissue.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.296 (book p.286) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":25,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-026","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Confirmation of a Meckel diverticulum can be difficult. Of the following, the MOsT sensitive study is","o":["plain abdominal radiographs","barium studies","radionuclide technetium-99m scans","abdominal ultrasound","abdominal CT scan"],"k":2,"ex":"The Meckel scan (technetium-99m pertechnetate) is the most sensitive test because ectopic gastric mucosa takes up the tracer.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nMucus-secreting cells of ectopic gastric mucosa take up pertechnetate, outlining the diverticulum; uptake can be enhanced with cimetidine/ranitidine, glucagon or pentagastrin. False negatives occur with anemia or absent gastric mucosa; false positives with intussusception, appendicitis, duplication cysts or vascular malformation.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Plain radiographs: non-specific.\n\n- B. Barium studies: the diverticulum rarely fills.\n- D. Ultrasound: may help occasionally but not sensitive.\n- E. CT: low yield for this diagnosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.296 (book p.286) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":26,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-028","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"In healthy full-term infants, meconium is passed within 48 hr of birth in","o":["99%","90%","80%","70%","60%"],"k":0,"ex":"Per the source, 99% of healthy full-term infants pass meconium within 48 hours.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nFailure to pass meconium within the first 24-48 hours suggests Hirschsprung disease, imperforate anus, meconium ileus (cystic fibrosis) or other obstruction.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. 90%: lower than the source figure.\n\n- C. 80%: lower than the source figure.\n- D. 70%: lower than the source figure.\n- E. 60%: lower than the source figure.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.296-297 (book p.286-287) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":28,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-030","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"The classic triad of intussusception, (pain, a palpable sausage-shaped abdominal mass, and bloody or currant jelly stool) is seen in","o":["s20% of patients","≤30% of patients","≤40% of patients","s50% of patients","≤60% of patients"],"k":1,"ex":"All three components of the classic triad are present together in fewer than 30% of children with intussusception (source: <30%).","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nThe triad is colicky pain, a palpable sausage-shaped mass and bloody (red currant jelly) stool. Red currant jelly stool is a late sign and the abdominal mass is found in only about 30% of patients, so the full triad cannot be common. Diagnosis therefore rests on suspicion, ultrasound (target/doughnut sign) and enema (claw sign), which is also therapeutic.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. <=20%: not the cut-off chosen by the source.\n\n- C. <=40%: not the cut-off chosen by the source.\n- D. <=50%: not the cut-off chosen by the source.\n- E. <=60%: not the cut-off chosen by the source (60% is roughly how often currant jelly stool occurs).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.297 (book p.287) | Answer PDF p.312 (book p.302)","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":30,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-035","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"One of the following features is MOrE common in ulcerative colitis than in Crohn disease","o":["abdominal pain","growth failure","rectal bleeding","mouth ulceration","strictures"],"k":2,"ex":"Bloody diarrhea/rectal bleeding is more characteristic of ulcerative colitis (diffuse mucosal inflammation of colon and rectum) than of Crohn disease.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nUlcerative colitis: superficial continuous inflammation of colon/rectum, bloody mucus diarrhea, tenesmus, crypt abscesses, pseudopolyps, toxic megacolon. Crohn disease: transmural skip lesions anywhere from mouth to anus, with abdominal pain, weight loss, growth failure, perianal disease, strictures and fistulae, and more extraintestinal manifestations.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Abdominal pain: classic presenting feature of Crohn disease.\n\n- B. Growth failure: more frequent in Crohn disease (less common in UC).\n- D. Mouth ulceration: aphthous ulcers occur with IBD, more with Crohn disease.\n- E. Strictures: transmural inflammation in Crohn disease causes strictures and fistulae.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.298 (book p.288) | Answer PDF p.313 (book p.303)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":35,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-037","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"The MosT common extraintestinal manifestation of celiac disease is","o":["osteoporosis","short stature","arthritis","iron-deficiency anemia","aphthous stomatitis"],"k":3,"ex":"Iron-deficiency anemia, often unresponsive to oral iron, is the most common extraintestinal manifestation of celiac disease.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nCeliac disease damages the proximal small bowel, where iron is absorbed. Other extraintestinal features: short stature and delayed puberty, osteopenia/osteoporosis (reversible in children on a gluten-free diet), arthritis, dermatitis herpetiformis, aphthous stomatitis and enamel defects, neurological problems (epilepsy with occipital calcifications).\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Osteoporosis: occurs but less commonly.\n\n- B. Short stature: occurs but less commonly.\n- C. Arthritis: occurs but less commonly.\n- E. Aphthous stomatitis: occurs but less commonly.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.298 (book p.288) | Answer PDF p.313 (book p.303)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":37,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-042","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Pathophysiology | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"In infants and very young toddlers chronic diarrhea can appear following infectious enteritis. The pathogenesis of the diarrhea is not always clear and may be related to","o":["food protein allergy","bacterial overgrowth","giardiasis","Strongyloides stercoralis","eosinophilic gastroenteropathy"],"k":0,"ex":"Mucosal damage after infectious enteritis can lead to secondary lactase deficiency and sensitisation to food proteins (cow's milk protein allergy), causing persistent diarrhea.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nThis is post-gastroenteritis syndrome: brush-border injury (for example rotavirus) reduces lactase, and the damaged mucosa can allow sensitisation to cow's milk protein; antibiotic-associated colitis is another cause. Management is lactose-free or hydrolyzed formula.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. Bacterial overgrowth: not the recognised post-enteritis mechanism in infants per the source.\n\n- C. Giardiasis: an infection that causes chronic diarrhea itself, not a sequel of enteritis.\n- D. Strongyloides stercoralis: a parasitic infection, not a post-enteritis mechanism.\n- E. Eosinophilic gastroenteropathy: a distinct condition, not a typical sequel.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.299 (book p.289) | Answer PDF p.313 (book p.303)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":42,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-043","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"Vitamin B12 and bile salts are only absorbed in","o":["distal ileum","duodenum","proximal jejunum","colon","proximal ileum"],"k":0,"ex":"Vitamin B12 (with intrinsic factor) and bile salts are absorbed in the terminal (distal) ileum.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nIleal disease or resection (Crohn disease, short bowel) therefore causes B12 deficiency (megaloblastic anemia) and bile-salt loss with fat malabsorption and diarrhea.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. Duodenum: absorbs iron and calcium.\n\n- C. Proximal jejunum: absorbs most nutrients, folate and carbohydrates.\n- D. Colon: absorbs water and electrolytes.\n- E. Proximal ileum: not the specific site of B12/bile-salt uptake.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.299 (book p.289) | Answer PDF p.313 (book p.303)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":43,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-045","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common congenital disorder associated with exocrine pancreatic insufficiency is","o":["Shwachman- Diamond syndrome","Johanson-Blizzard syndrome","Pearson bone marrow syndrome","isolated pancreatic enzyme deficiency","cystic fibrosis"],"k":4,"ex":"Cystic fibrosis is the most common congenital cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nThick secretions obstruct pancreatic ducts, causing fat and protein malabsorption (steatorrhea, failure to thrive).\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Shwachman-Diamond syndrome: second most common; neutropenia and skeletal dysplasia.\n\n- B. Johanson-Blizzard syndrome: rare; hypoplastic alae nasi, deafness.\n- C. Pearson syndrome: rare; sideroblastic anemia with pancreatic dysfunction.\n- D. Isolated enzyme deficiency: rare.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.300 (book p.290) | Answer PDF p.314 (book p.304)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":45,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-051","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Guillain-Barré syndrome can be a sequel in the following foodborne bacterial illness","o":["Bacillus anthracis","Bacillus cereus","Brucella abortus","Campylobacter jejuni","Clostridium botulinum"],"k":3,"ex":"Campylobacter jejuni gastroenteritis can be followed by Guillain-Barre syndrome 1-3 weeks later.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nMolecular mimicry between Campylobacter surface antigens and nerve gangliosides leads to a post-infectious autoimmune demyelinating polyneuropathy.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Bacillus anthracis: anthrax; not linked to GBS.\n\n- B. Bacillus cereus: toxin food poisoning.\n- C. Brucella abortus: brucellosis (fever, hepatosplenomegaly, arthritis).\n- E. Clostridium botulinum: botulism gives toxin-mediated descending paralysis, a mimic rather than a post-infectious cause.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.301 (book p.291) | Answer PDF p.314 (book p.304)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":51,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-055","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Risks associated with severe dehydration that might necessitate intravenous resuscitation include","o":["age<9mo","postmaturity","fever >38°C if 3-36 mo of age","secretary diarrhea","depressed level of consciousness"],"k":4,"ex":"A depressed level of consciousness is a sign of shock, which requires intravenous resuscitation.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nSevere dehydration/shock is recognised by depressed consciousness, pale or mottled cold extremities, weak pulses, capillary refill >2 s, tachycardia, hypotension (late). These patients need an IV bolus of 10-20 mL/kg normal saline (intraosseous if no IV access).\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Age <9 months: the risk threshold in the curriculum and source is <6 months.\n\n- B. Postmaturity: prematurity and low birth weight raise the risk, not postmaturity.\n- C. Fever >38 C at 3-36 months: the source threshold is >39 C (>38 C applies below 3 months).\n- D. Secretory diarrhea: not a listed risk (bloody diarrhea is).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.301-302 (book p.291-292) | Answer PDF p.314-315 (book p.304-305)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":55,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-056","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"[beginning of stem missing in OCR] (ORS) has the following per liter EXCEPT","o":["75 mEq of sodium","50 mEq of chloride","20 mEq of potassium","75 mmol of glucose","total osmolarity of 245 m0sm"],"k":1,"ex":"WHO low-osmolarity ORS contains about 65 mEq/L chloride (source: 64), so 50 mEq/L is the false component.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nThe reduced-osmolarity formula: Na 75, Cl 65, K 20 mEq/L, glucose 75 mmol/L, citrate 10 mmol/L, osmolarity 245 mOsm/L. Compared with the old formula (Na 90, Cl 80, glucose 111, 311 mOsm/L) it reduces stool output, vomiting and the need for IV fluids. Sodium-glucose co-transport pulls Na and water across the gut even when secretion is high.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. 75 mEq/L sodium: correct.\n\n- C. 20 mEq/L potassium: correct.\n- D. 75 mmol/L glucose: correct.\n- E. 245 mOsm/L: correct total osmolarity.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.302 (book p.292) | Answer PDF p.315 (book p.305)","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":56,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-058","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"Continued enteral feeding in diarrhea aids in recovery from the episode, but the following should be avoided","o":["rice","potatoes","lean meats","fruits","juices"],"k":4,"ex":"Fruit juices (and other drinks high in simple sugars) should be avoided because their high osmolarity worsens osmotic diarrhea.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nEarly, continued feeding speeds recovery: breast milk, usual diet, rice, potatoes, lean meat and fruit are acceptable. Juices and carbonated drinks, which are high in sugar and low in sodium, and fatty foods should be avoided.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Rice: appropriate.\n\n- B. Potatoes: appropriate.\n- C. Lean meats: appropriate.\n- D. Fruits: whole fruit is acceptable; it is fruit juice that is discouraged.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.302 (book p.292) | Answer PDF p.315 (book p.305)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":58,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-063","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"Increased fecal calprotectin concentration is seen in","o":["fat malabsorption","carbohydrate malabsorption","intestinal inflammation","pancreatic function","reduced intestinal surface"],"k":2,"ex":"Fecal calprotectin rises with intestinal (neutrophilic) inflammation, such as IBD.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nIt helps separate IBD from functional disorders and monitors activity. Digestive-absorptive tests (fat, carbohydrate, pancreatic function) point to small-bowel or pancreatic disease, whereas calprotectin or lactoferrin point to colitis.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Fat malabsorption: shown by stool fat tests.\n\n- B. Carbohydrate malabsorption: shown by reducing substances, pH, hydrogen breath test.\n- D. Pancreatic function: assessed by fecal elastase.\n- E. Reduced intestinal surface: villous atrophy and absorption tests.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.303 (book p.293) | Answer PDF p.315 (book p.305)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":63,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-064","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"The following are alarm symptoms of functional abdominal pain ExCEPT","o":["persistent left lower quadrant pain","unexplained fever","genitourinary tract symptoms","dysphagia","nocturnal diarrhea"],"k":0,"ex":"Persistent left lower quadrant pain is not among the listed alarm features, which name right upper or right lower quadrant pain.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nAlarm features suggesting organic disease: persistent pain away from the umbilicus (RUQ or RLQ), fever, weight loss, night waking, GI bleeding, persistent vomiting, dysphagia, diarrhea including nocturnal, genitourinary symptoms, perianal disease, family history of IBD, celiac or ulcer disease.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. Unexplained fever: alarm feature.\n\n- C. Genitourinary symptoms: suggest UTI, an alarm feature.\n- D. Dysphagia: eosinophilic esophagitis, an alarm feature.\n- E. Nocturnal diarrhea: alarm feature.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.303 (book p.293) | Answer PDF p.315 (book p.305)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":64,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-068","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Differential Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"The clinical presentation of one of the following closely mimics appendicitis","o":["Meckel diverticulitis","mesenteric adenitis","sickle cell disease","pancreatitis","ovarian torsion"],"k":0,"ex":"Per the source, Meckel diverticulitis closely mimics appendicitis; the diagnosis is usually made at surgery.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nAn inflamed Meckel diverticulum gives right-lower-quadrant pain, vomiting and fever like acute appendicitis. Other conditions (mesenteric adenitis, pancreatitis, ovarian torsion, sickle cell crisis) also enter the differential of an acute abdomen but are not described by the source as closely mimicking it.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. Mesenteric adenitis: also mimics appendicitis (see verification note) but with milder pain, often after URTI, and symptoms settle in 24-48 hours.\n\n- C. Sickle cell disease: vaso-occlusive abdominal pain, part of the acute abdomen differential.\n- D. Pancreatitis: epigastric pain with vomiting, part of the differential.\n- E. Ovarian torsion: pelvic/lower abdominal pain in girls, part of the differential.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.304 (book p.294) | Answer PDF p.315 (book p.305)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":68,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-077","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"One of the following rules out biliary atresia","o":["history of prematurity","normal size of liver","normal consistency of liver","consistently pigmented stools","no familial incidence"],"k":3,"ex":"Complete bile-duct obstruction produces persistently pale (acholic) stools, so consistently pigmented stools argue against biliary atresia (as does bile-stained duodenal fluid).","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nBiliary atresia presents as neonatal cholestasis: jaundice, dark urine, pale stools, hepatomegaly, raised ALP and GGT, a contracted or absent gallbladder on ultrasound, no excretion on HIDA scan.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. History of prematurity: does not exclude the diagnosis.\n\n- B. Normal liver size: hepatomegaly is typical but a normal size does not exclude early disease.\n- C. Normal liver consistency: does not exclude early disease.\n- E. No family history: biliary atresia is usually sporadic (etiology unknown).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.306 (book p.296) | Answer PDF p.316 (book p.306)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":77,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-078","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Treatment | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"[beginning of stem missing in OCR] higher (90%) if performed before","o":["4 wk of life","6 wk of life","8 wk of life","10 wk of life","12 wk of life"],"k":2,"ex":"The Kasai operation restores good bile flow in about 90% when performed before 8 weeks (about 60 days) of age.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nSuccess falls the later the operation is done, so infants with prolonged conjugated jaundice or pale stools need urgent referral. Liver transplantation is considered if the Kasai procedure fails.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. 4 weeks: earlier than the threshold quoted by the source and curriculum.\n\n- B. 6 weeks: earlier than the threshold quoted by the source and curriculum.\n- D. 10 weeks: beyond the window; success is lower.\n- E. 12 weeks: beyond the window; success is lower.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.306 (book p.296) | Answer PDF p.316 (book p.306)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":78,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-080","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Persistence of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia at levels >20 mg/dL after the 1st wk of life in the absence of hemolysis should suggest","o":["galactosemia","tyrosinemia","α1-Antitrypsin deficiency","Crigler-Najjar type 1","Maple serum urine disease"],"k":3,"ex":"Severe, persistent unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia after the first week without hemolysis suggests Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nType I (autosomal recessive) has absent glucuronyl transferase, causes kernicterus, and does not respond to phenobarbitone; it is treated with phototherapy, exchange transfusion and eventually liver transplantation. Type II has partial enzyme deficiency and responds to phenobarbitone.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Galactosemia: conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with liver dysfunction.\n\n- B. Tyrosinemia: neonatal cholestasis/liver failure.\n- C. Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency: conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (neonatal cholestasis).\n- E. 'Maple serum urine disease' (as printed): maple syrup urine disease presents with encephalopathy and ketoacidosis, not isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.306 (book p.296) | Answer PDF p.316 (book p.306)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":80,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-085","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"No parenteral transmission occur in the following hepatotropic viruses","o":["HAV","HBV","HCV","HOV","HEV"],"k":4,"ex":"Per the source, HEV is never transmitted parenterally (hepatitis E is fecal-oral only).","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nHBV, HCV and HDV are transmitted parenterally and sexually; HAV and HEV are fecal-oral. The source notes that parenteral spread of HAV is rare but possible, so only HEV is 'never'.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. HAV: fecal-oral; parenteral spread rare but reported (source).\n\n- B. HBV: parenteral, sexual and perinatal.\n- C. HCV: parenteral.\n- D. 'HOV' (as printed in the OCR; the source explanation refers to HDV): HDV is parenteral.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.307 (book p.297) | Answer PDF p.316 (book p.306)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":85,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-087","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"[beginning of stem missing in OCR] following EXCEPT","o":["intravenous drug","contaminated needles","sexual contact","intimate contact with carriers","sharing toys"],"k":4,"ex":"Sharing toys is indirect, casual exposure and is not a route of HBV transmission (source).","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nHBV spreads through blood and body fluids: injection drug use and contaminated needles, sexual contact, close contact with carriers (mucosal or skin-break exposure), and perinatal transmission.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Intravenous drug use: percutaneous route.\n\n- B. Contaminated needles: percutaneous route.\n- C. Sexual contact: recognised route.\n- D. Intimate contact with carriers: recognised route (source).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.307-308 (book p.297-298) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":87,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-089","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Routine screening for H8V infection requires assay of multiple serologic markers, all the following are true EXCEPT","o":["HBsAg is the first serologic marker of infection to appear","Anti-HBc is a valuable serologic marker of acute HBV infection","anti-HBs and anti- HBc are detected in persons immunized with hepatitis B vaccine","HBeAg is a marker of infectivity","HBsAg levels fall before symptoms wane"],"k":2,"ex":"A vaccinated person has only anti-HBs; anti-HBc appears after natural infection, so statement C is false.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nHBsAg appears first and marks infection; IgM anti-HBc marks acute infection; anti-HBs plus anti-HBc IgG means resolved infection; anti-HBs alone means vaccination (the vaccine contains only HBsAg); HBeAg reflects viral replication and infectivity.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. True: HBsAg is the first serologic marker to appear.\n\n- B. True: IgM anti-HBc marks acute infection.\n- D. True: HBeAg indicates infectivity (the curriculum slide writes 'HBc Ag' for replication/infectivity).\n- E. True per the source: HBsAg falls as symptoms resolve.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.308 (book p.298) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":89,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-090","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Other | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"HBV is usually spread by","o":["breastfeeding","kissing","hugging","sharing utensils","sharing needles"],"k":4,"ex":"Sharing needles is a parenteral route; HBV is not spread by breastfeeding, kissing, hugging or sharing utensils.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nHBV is transmitted by blood and body fluids (needles, sexual contact, perinatal). Casual contact does not transmit it.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Breastfeeding: no greater risk than formula feeding (source).\n\n- B. Kissing: not a route.\n- C. Hugging: not a route.\n- D. Sharing utensils: not a route.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.308 (book p.298) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":90,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-091","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common hepatobiliary disease associated with inflammatory bowel disease (1BD) is","o":["hepatic abscess","portal vein thrombosis","sclerosing cholangitis","autoimmune hepatitis","biliary carcinoma"],"k":2,"ex":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis is the most common hepatobiliary disease associated with IBD, especially ulcerative colitis.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nIt is suspected with cholestatic enzyme elevation; ursodeoxycholic acid is used for treatment. Autoimmune hepatitis also occurs with IBD but less commonly.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Hepatic abscess: not the typical association.\n\n- B. Portal vein thrombosis: not the typical association.\n- D. Autoimmune hepatitis: associated but less common.\n- E. Biliary carcinoma: a rare complication of long-standing sclerosing cholangitis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.308 (book p.298) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":91,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-092","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Total parenteral nutrition (TpN) can cause a variety of liver diseases. Of the following, the MOsT severe complication is","o":["hepatic steatosis","gallbladder damage","bile duct damage","cholestasis","sclerosing cholangitis"],"k":3,"ex":"Cholestasis is the most severe TPN-associated liver complication; it can progress to fibrosis and cirrhosis.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nRisk factors: prolonged TPN, prematurity, low birth weight, sepsis, NEC and short bowel. It is a major limitation to long-term TPN.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Hepatic steatosis: common but milder and reversible.\n\n- B. Gallbladder damage: sludge or stones, less severe.\n- C. Bile duct damage: not the main severe complication.\n- E. Sclerosing cholangitis: not the typical TPN complication.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.308-309 (book p.298-299) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":92,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-093","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Diagnosis | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Autoimmune hepatitis is a clinical diagnosis based on certain diagnostic criteria; no single test will make this diagnosis. Important positive features include the following EXCEPT","o":["female gender","primary elevation in alkaline phosphatase","elevated y-globulin levels","presence of autoantibodies","characteristic histologic findings"],"k":1,"ex":"In autoimmune hepatitis the primary elevation is of transaminases, not alkaline phosphatase; a primary ALP rise suggests a cholestatic disorder.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nPositive features: female predominance, hypergammaglobulinemia (IgG), autoantibodies, typical histology, low complement. It responds to prednisolone and azathioprine. Sclerosing cholangitis, treated with ursodeoxycholic acid, may overlap.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Female gender: more common in girls.\n\n- C. Elevated gamma-globulin: diagnostic feature.\n- D. Autoantibodies: diagnostic feature.\n- E. Characteristic histology: diagnostic feature.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.309 (book p.299) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":93,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-096","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"The MosT common presentation of portal hypertension is","o":["jaundice","splenomegaly","esophageal hemorrhage","ascites","dilated cutaneous collateral vessels in the periumbilical region"],"k":2,"ex":"Bleeding from esophageal varices is the most common presentation of portal hypertension (source).","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nSplenomegaly with hypersplenism often comes next and may be found first on routine examination. Ascites and dilated collateral veins also occur, and jaundice reflects liver disease rather than portal hypertension itself.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Jaundice: a sign of liver dysfunction.\n\n- B. Splenomegaly: next most common feature (source).\n- D. Ascites: occurs in cirrhosis but less often the first presentation.\n- E. Periumbilical collateral veins (caput medusae): possible but not the most common.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.309 (book p.299) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":96,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-097","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management | **Explanation depth:** Short","q":"The MosT common indication for liver transplantation in children is","o":["Alagille syndrome","congenital hepatic fibrosis","hepatocellular carcinoma","biliary atresia","Wilson disease"],"k":3,"ex":"Biliary atresia is the most common indication for liver transplantation in children, particularly after a failed Kasai operation.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nMetabolic and inborn disorders, autoimmune and familial cholestatic disorders and acute hepatic necrosis follow it.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- A. Alagille syndrome: less common.\n\n- B. Congenital hepatic fibrosis: managed mainly by treating portal hypertension.\n- C. Hepatocellular carcinoma: rare in children.\n- E. Wilson disease: transplantation for acute liver failure or end-stage disease.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.309 (book p.299) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":97,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GITACCEPTE-100","spec":"git","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Complications | **Explanation depth:** Moderate","q":"Acute secondary peritonitis most often results from entry of enteric bacteria into the peritoneal cavity through a necrotic defect in the wall of the intestines or other viscus. It MOsT commonly follows","o":["perforation of the appendix","rupture of a Meckel diverticulum","intussusception","peptic ulceration","necrotizing enterocolitis"],"k":0,"ex":"Perforation of the appendix is the most common cause of secondary peritonitis in children.","more":"Clinical / scientific reasoning:\nAppendicitis is the commonest surgical abdominal emergency; young children perforate quickly because the omentum is less developed. Treatment is laparotomy, appendectomy and peritoneal lavage.\n\nWhy other options are incorrect:\n- B. Rupture of a Meckel diverticulum: less common.\n\n- C. Intussusception: perforation or gangrene of the bowel can occur late.\n- D. Peptic ulceration: perforation is uncommon in children.\n- E. Necrotizing enterocolitis: mainly neonatal.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.310 (book p.300) | Answer PDF p.317 (book p.307)","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Gastrointestinal Tract","nlNumber":100,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-019","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"For children <5 yr, the highest global prevalence of micronutrient and trace elements deficiencies is that of","o":["vitamin A deficiency","zinc deficiency","iron deficiency .jodine deficiency","copper deficiency"],"k":0,"ex":"The question asks which micronutrient deficiency has the highest global prevalence in children under 5 years. Using the figures quoted in the Nelson key, vitamin A deficiency (~33%) exceeds iodine (~29%), iron-deficiency anemia (~18%) and zinc (~17%) deficiency.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): vitamin A deficiency, ~33% per the key; causes xerophthalmia, night blindness, keratomalacia and increased infectious mortality.\n\n- **B.** Zinc deficiency (~17%): causes growth faltering, diarrhea/pneumonia susceptibility and acrodermatitis-like rash; not the highest.\n- **C.** Iron deficiency: the commonest single nutritional deficiency in many reports; the key quotes ~18% for iron-deficiency anemia in this age group (OCR: letter C present, text on separate line).\n- **D.** Iodine deficiency (~29%): goiter, hypothyroidism, cretinism; second in the key's ranking (OCR shows a stray leading '.' before 'jodine').\n- **E.** Copper deficiency: rare (prematurity, TPN, Menkes); not a major global problem.\n\nNelson explanation:\n19.(A). For children <5 yr, the global prevalence is estimated to be 33% for vitamin A\ndeficiency, 29% for jodine deficiency, 17% for zinc deficiency, and 18% for iron\ndeficiency anemia. Prevalence of micronutrient deficiencies tends to be highest in\nAfrica.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification (mild): ranking depends on the survey; WHO and other sources usually rank iron-deficiency (anemia) highest. The source key is preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 56 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":19,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-029","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Management","q":"The aim of the rehabilitation phase in the treatment of malnutrition is to","o":["repair cellular function","correct fluid and electrolyte imbalance","restore homeostasis . catch-up growth","prevent death from infection"],"k":3,"ex":"Treatment of severe acute malnutrition has phases. The stabilization phase (first days to ~1 week) treats life-threatening problems: hypoglycemia, hypothermia, dehydration, electrolyte imbalance, infection, and restores homeostasis/cellular function.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Repair of cellular function: a goal of the stabilization phase.\n\n- **B.** Correction of fluid and electrolyte imbalance: stabilization phase.\n- **C.** Restoring homeostasis: stabilization phase.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): catch-up growth, restoring wasted tissue, is the aim of rehabilitation (OCR: a stray '.' replaces the letter D).\n- **E.** Preventing death from infection: stabilization phase (antibiotics for all severe cases).\n\nNelson explanation:\n29.(D). The aim of the rehabilitation phase is to restore wasted tissues (i.e., catch-up\ngrowth).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 58 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":29,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-036","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are childhood obesity-associated comorbidities later in life ExCEPT","o":["hypertension","gallbladder disease","tibia vara","asthma","type 1 diabetes mellitus"],"k":4,"ex":"Childhood obesity has metabolic, cardiovascular, hepatic, orthopedic, respiratory and psychological complications. The stem asks which condition is NOT associated.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Hypertension: a recognized comorbidity.\n\n- **B.** Gallbladder disease (gallstones): recognized, especially with rapid weight loss (OCR: letter B missing before this option).\n- **C.** Tibia vara (Blount disease): bowing from excess mechanical load on the medial proximal tibial physis.\n- **D.** Asthma: recognized association with obesity.\n- **E.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): type 1 diabetes mellitus. The associated condition is type 2 diabetes (the key states this).\n\nNelson explanation:\n36.(E). Type 2 diabetes mellitus is a childhood obesity-associated comorbidity.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 59 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":36,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-041","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The only Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved medication for obesity in children <16 yr old is","o":["orlistat","phentermine","topiramate","amylin","lorcaserin"],"k":0,"ex":"Orlistat is a gastrointestinal lipase inhibitor that reduces dietary fat absorption, giving modest weight loss; side effects are flatulence, oily stools/spotting, urgency and reduced absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. The Nelson text of that time named it as the only FDA-approved drug for obesity in adolescents (age 12 and above).","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): orlistat, a lipase inhibitor.\n\n- **B.** Phentermine: sympathomimetic anorectic approved for short-term use in adults/older adolescents, not the keyed drug.\n- **C.** Topiramate: antiepileptic that causes weight loss; used off-label (and later in a phentermine-topiramate combination).\n- **D.** Amylin (pramlintide): diabetes drug, not approved for pediatric obesity.\n- **E.** Lorcaserin: serotonin 2C agonist, adult drug, withdrawn from the US market in 2020.\n\nNelson explanation:\n41.(A). Orlistat decreases absorption of fat, resulting in modest weight loss.\nComplications include flatulence, oily stools, and spotting.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required (outdated): since this key was written, liraglutide and semaglutide (adolescents ≥12 y), phentermine-topiramate ER (≥12 y) and setmelanotide (genetic obesity, ≥6 y) have been FDA-approved for pediatric obesity, so 'only approved' is no longer true; verify current labeling. Source answer preserved.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 60 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":41,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-071","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"Radiographic features of scurvy are similar to that of","o":["copper deficiency","magnesium deficiency","vitamin A excess","vitamin D excess . mercury exposure"],"k":0,"ex":"Scurvy (vitamin C deficiency) causes defective collagen: subperiosteal hemorrhage, metaphyseal 'white line' of Frankel, ground-glass osteopenia, Wimberger ring and corner (Pelkan) spurs. Copper deficiency (e.g., Menkes disease, prematurity, long TPN) produces very similar radiographs: osteopenia, metaphyseal spurring/cupping and periosteal new bone, which can also mimic child abuse.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): copper deficiency – metaphyseal spurs, osteopenia and periosteal reaction resemble scurvy.\n\n- **B.** Magnesium deficiency: causes hypocalcemia/tetany, no characteristic scurvy-like bone picture.\n- **C.** Vitamin A excess: causes periosteal new bone/cortical hyperostosis, but not the metaphyseal findings of scurvy.\n- **D.** Vitamin D excess: dense metaphyseal bands and soft-tissue calcification; not the scurvy pattern.\n- **E.** Mercury exposure (acrodynia/pink disease): painful extremities and rash; not a radiographic match (OCR: letter E missing, stray '.').\n\nNelson explanation:\n71.(A).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"The source answer key has no explanation text; the answer is accepted as the best match with medical knowledge.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 65 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":71,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-074","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Ingestion of large doses of vitamin C can cause","o":["musculoskeletal pain","osmotic diarrhea","severe headache .respiratory symptoms","hematemesis"],"k":1,"ex":"Vitamin C is water-soluble, so excess is excreted, but very large doses (several grams) cause osmotic diarrhea, abdominal cramps and nausea; they may also raise urinary oxalate (stone risk) and interfere with glucose and stool-occult-blood tests. The key states the GI problems of osmotic diarrhea and abdominal pain.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Musculoskeletal pain: occurs in scurvy (deficiency), not excess.\n\n- **B.** Correct (source answer): osmotic diarrhea (with abdominal pain).\n- **C.** Severe headache: not a feature of vitamin C excess (typical of vitamin A excess/pseudotumor).\n- **D.** Respiratory symptoms: not an effect (OCR: letter D missing, stray '.').\n- **E.** Hematemesis: bleeding is a deficiency feature (scurvy), not of excess.\n\nNelson explanation:\n74.(B). Larger doses of vitamin C can cause gastrointestinal problems, such as\nabdominal pain and osmotic diarrhea.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 66 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":74,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-075","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The following are recognized causes of craniotabes ExCEPT","o":["osteogenesis imperfect","rickets","syphilis","Sotos syndrome","normal newborn"],"k":3,"ex":"Craniotabes is a soft, compressible skull (ping-pong ball sensation over the occiput/parietal bones) caused by thinning or demineralization of the skull. Causes: rickets (first sign), osteogenesis imperfecta, congenital syphilis, hydrocephalus, prematurity, and it can be normal in newborns.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Osteogenesis imperfecta: cause (bone fragility with wormian bones).\n\n- **B.** Rickets: classic cause, often the first skull sign.\n- **C.** Syphilis (congenital): cause (periostitis/osteochondritis).\n- **D.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): Sotos syndrome causes a large head/overgrowth, not craniotabes (as in the key).\n- **E.** Normal newborn: physiological craniotabes near the lambdoid suture (especially preterm).\n\nNelson explanation:\n75.(D). Soto's syndrome causes large head.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 66 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":75,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-076","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are recognized causes of rickets ExCEPT","o":["Mccune-Albright syndrome","epidermal nevus syndrome","tuberus sclerosis","neurofibromatosis","aluminum-containing antacids"],"k":2,"ex":"Rickets can be nutritional or caused by renal phosphate wasting or phosphate binding. Aluminum-containing antacids bind intestinal phosphate and cause phosphate depletion.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** McCune-Albright syndrome: causes renal phosphate loss (FGF23 from fibrous dysplasia).\n\n- **B.** Epidermal nevus syndrome: hypophosphatemic rickets/osteomalacia.\n- **C.** Correct answer (EXCEPT): tuberous sclerosis is not a recognized cause of rickets.\n- **D.** Neurofibromatosis: can cause hypophosphatemic rickets through renal phosphate wasting.\n- **E.** Aluminum-containing antacids: bind phosphate in the gut (phosphorus deficiency).\n\nNelson explanation:\n76.(C). Aluminum-containing antacids cause phosphorus deficiency. McCune-Albright\nsyndrome, epidermal nevus syndrome, and Neurofibromatosis enhance renal losses.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 66 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":76,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-077","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Dominant features in X-linked hypophasphatemic rickets are","o":["head; craniotabes, frontal bossing, and caries","back; scoliosis, lordosis, and kyphosis","chest; rachitic rosary and Harrison groove","extremities; coxa vara and windswept deformity","general; listlessness and failure to thrive"],"k":3,"ex":"X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets (XLH) is an X-linked dominant disorder of renal phosphate wasting (PHEX gene, raised FGF23). It presents after the first year with short stature and marked lower-limb deformity (coxa vara, genu varum/valgum, windswept deformity), with relatively little skull and chest involvement and rare tetany; it does not respond to ordinary doses of vitamin D.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Head features (craniotabes, frontal bossing, caries): classic of nutritional rickets; minimal in XLH (though dental abscesses can occur).\n\n- **B.** Spine (scoliosis, lordosis, kyphosis): non-specific, not dominant.\n- **C.** Chest (rachitic rosary, Harrison groove): nutritional rickets; minimal in XLH.\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): extremities – coxa vara and windswept deformity dominate.\n- **E.** General features (listlessness, failure to thrive): non-specific; seen more in nutritional rickets.\n\nNelson explanation:\n77.(D).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 66 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":77,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-078","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Inorganic phosphorus in rickets is raised in","o":["vitamin D deficiency vitamin D dependent rickets","dietary calcium deficiency BX- linked hypophosphatemic rickets","Fanconi syndrome hypophosphatasia","chronic renal failure dietary calcium deficiency Evitamin D deficiency","tumor-induced rickets 56 [Continuation on source page 67]"],"k":3,"ex":"In rickets serum phosphorus is usually low because of secondary hyperparathyroidism (vitamin D or calcium deficiency) or renal phosphate wasting (Fanconi, hypophosphatemic rickets, tumor-induced). In chronic renal failure (renal osteodystrophy) reduced GFR retains phosphate, so serum phosphorus is raised.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Vitamin D deficiency: low phosphorus (PTH-driven phosphaturia).\n\n- **B.** Dietary calcium deficiency: low phosphorus through secondary hyperparathyroidism (OCR: letter B lost; option appears as 'dietary calcium deficiency').\n- **C.** Fanconi syndrome: low phosphorus (proximal tubular wasting).\n- **D.** Correct (source answer): chronic renal failure – phosphate retention, high serum phosphorus.\n- **E.** Tumor-induced rickets: low phosphorus (FGF23 excess).\n\nNelson explanation:\n78.(D). All other distracters are associated with decreased level of inorganic\nphosphorus.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR contamination: the question block contains an unrelated second option list after the page marker ('vitamin D dependent rickets / X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets / hypophosphatasia / dietary calcium deficiency / vitamin D deficiency'); it appears to belong to another question and is not analysed here. Check PDF pages 66–67.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 66 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":78,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-081","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Urinary phosphorus is reduced in","o":["chronic renal failure","autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets","autosomal recessive hypophosphatemic rickets","dietary calcium deficiency","Fanconi syndrome"],"k":0,"ex":"Urinary phosphate excretion is high in conditions with renal phosphate wasting or PTH excess (Fanconi syndrome, hypophosphatemic rickets, dietary calcium or vitamin D deficiency with secondary hyperparathyroidism) and low when GFR is reduced (chronic renal failure) or phosphorus intake is deficient. The key states urinary phosphorus is reduced only in CKD and dietary phosphorus deficiency.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): chronic renal failure – reduced filtered phosphate, low urinary excretion (serum phosphorus is high).\n\n- **B.** Autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets (FGF23): renal phosphate wasting, high urinary phosphate.\n- **C.** Autosomal recessive hypophosphatemic rickets (DMP1/ENPP1): renal phosphate wasting.\n- **D.** Dietary calcium deficiency: secondary hyperparathyroidism causes phosphaturia (OCR: letter D lost).\n- **E.** Fanconi syndrome: generalized proximal tubular loss, high urinary phosphate.\n\nNelson explanation:\n81.(A). Reduced in chronic kidney disease and dietary phosphorus deficiency only while\nelevated in all other forms of rickets.\n63\nM\n[Source page 74]","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"OCR: page markers ('63', 'M', '[Source page 74]') are interleaved with the answer text; the source explanation reads as printed.\nNelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 67 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":81,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-082","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The following hereditary forms of rickets may help to diagnose an apparently healthy mother with the same disease","o":["X- linked hypophosphatemic rickets","autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets","autosomal recessive hypophosphatemic rickets","Fanconi syndrome","hereditary hypophosphatemic rickets with hypercalciuria"],"k":0,"ex":"X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets is X-linked dominant with variable expressivity, so a mother may have only mild or undiagnosed hypophosphatemia (low serum phosphate, short stature, bowed legs) and be diagnosed after her affected child. Recessive forms usually have unaffected carrier parents; Fanconi syndrome has many causes; autosomal dominant forms (FGF23) show incomplete penetrance.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Correct (source answer): XLH – an undiagnosed mild case in the mother is not unusual.\n\n- **B.** Autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets: variable penetrance; the key does not select it.\n- **C.** Autosomal recessive hypophosphatemic rickets: parents are asymptomatic carriers.\n- **D.** Fanconi syndrome: acquired or genetic proximal tubulopathy; not the answer.\n- **E.** Hereditary hypophosphatemic rickets with hypercalciuria (recessive): carriers usually normal.\n\nNelson explanation:\n82.(A). Undiagnosed disease in the mother is not unusual in X-linked\nhypophosphatemia.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 67 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":82,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-GROWTHNUTR-087","spec":"growth","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The first factor to be affected by deficiency of vitamin K is","o":["Factor 1","Factor II","Factor VII","Factor IX","Factor X"],"k":2,"ex":"Vitamin K-dependent factors are II, VII, IX and X (plus proteins C and S). Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 4–6 hours), so it falls first and the prothrombin time prolongs first in vitamin K deficiency or warfarin therapy.","more":"Point-by-point analysis:\n- **A.** Factor I (fibrinogen): not vitamin K-dependent.\n\n- **B.** Factor II (prothrombin): vitamin K-dependent but half-life ~60 h; falls later.\n- **C.** Correct (source answer): Factor VII, shortest half-life.\n- **D.** Factor IX: half-life ~24 h; falls later.\n- **E.** Factor X: half-life ~40 h; falls later.\n\nNelson explanation:\n87.(C). Factor Vil has the shortest half life of the coagulation factors.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Nelson's key is recorded without alteration. Independent medical verification and original-PDF visual checking have not been completed in this processing draft.\nSource file is marked as a processing draft; retained as requested and flagged for review.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Nutrition · question page 68 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Growth and Nutrition","nlNumber":87,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-001","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The hematologic features of congenital hypoplastic anemia (Diamond-Blackfan Anemia) include all the following EXCEPT","o":["normocytic anemia","reticulocytopenia","deficiency or absence of red blood cell bone marrow precursors","elevated fetal hemoglobin (Hb F)","elevated serum iron levels"],"k":0,"ex":"Diamond–Blackfan anemia (DBA) is a pure red cell aplasia presenting in infancy, usually at 2–3 months (25% at birth). The CBC shows low hemoglobin with a high MCV, so the red cells are macrocytic for age, not normocytic.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nReticulocytopenia and absent red cell precursors in the marrow are the defining findings of red cell aplasia. Hb F is elevated (Nelson), and serum iron is normal to high because the marrow is not using it (Nelson).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.356 · Answer PDF p.372 · **Type:** Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":1,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-004","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The main stay of treatment for congenital hypoplastic anemia is","o":["corticosteroids","androgen","antithymocyte globulin (ATG)","fully matched-related stem cell transplantation","fully matched-unrelated stem cell transplantation"],"k":0,"ex":"Corticosteroids are the first-line drug treatment of DBA and help about three quarters of patients (Nelson). Patients who do not respond depend on chronic transfusion (with iron chelation), and stem cell transplantation is reserved for steroid non-responders with long-term transfusion needs.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nAndrogens are used in Fanconi anemia (curriculum), not DBA. ATG is a treatment of acquired aplastic anemia rather than red cell aplasia of DBA. Matched-related transplantation is the curative option for non-responders, and results with matched-unrelated or partly mismatched donors are inferior (Nelson), so neither is the mainstay.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.356 · Answer PDF p.372 · **Type:** Treatment · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":4,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-006","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The valuable diagnostic feature that differentiate anemia of chronic disorder from iron deficiency anemia is","o":["low serum iron","low or normal serum transferrin","normal bone marrow cellularity","normochromic normocytic RBC","leukocytosis"],"k":1,"ex":"Serum iron is low in both conditions, but total iron-binding capacity (transferrin) is high in iron deficiency and low or normal in anemia of chronic disease; ferritin is low in iron deficiency and normal or high in chronic disease, and marrow iron stores are absent in iron deficiency and present in chronic disease.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nLow serum iron is shared by both. Marrow cellularity and leukocyte count do not separate them. Red cells can be normocytic in chronic disease but are also frequently microcytic, so cell appearance is not a reliable discriminator.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.357 · Answer PDF p.372 · *Question number lost in OCR; `[restored by sequence and key]`* · **Type:** Differential Diagnosis · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":6,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-007","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"All the following are true regarding physiologic anemia of infancy EXCEPT","o":["it is exaggerated in premature infants","the hemoglobin concentration range is between 9-11 g/dL","the hemoglobin concentration rarely falls below 10 g/dL in healthy infants","vitamin E deficiency does not play a role","iron deficiency is a contributing factor"],"k":4,"ex":"Hemoglobin falls from the high neonatal level (about 14–24 g/dL) to about 10–11 g/dL at 8–12 weeks because the end of intrauterine hypoxia switches off erythropoietin and red cells have a short life span. Iron deficiency is not the cause: iron recycled from fetal red cells keeps stores sufficient until about 20 weeks, so E is false.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nIt is more marked in preterm infants (low red cell survival, low stores, rapid growth). The stated hemoglobin range and the statement that it rarely falls below 10 g/dL describe the usual nadir (the curriculum gives 10–11 g/dL; Nelson's range is slightly wider). Vitamin E deficiency is not a contributor in the physiologic form (Nelson). Term infants need no treatment, whereas preterm infants need iron supplements.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.357 · Answer PDF p.372 · **Type:** Pathophysiology · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":7,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-008","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Folic acid deficiency may develop in the following children ExCEPT","o":["a term infant fed on goat's milk","an infant fed on unfortified powdered milk","an infant fed on pasteurized cow's milk","a child with chronic use of phenytoin anticonvulsant","a child with trimethoprim prophylaxis for recurrent urinary tract infection"],"k":2,"ex":"Folate comes from the diet and body stores last only 2–4 months. Human milk, pasteurized cow's milk and infant formulas supply adequate folate, so pasteurized cow's milk is the exception.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nGoat's milk and unfortified powdered milk are low in folate. Phenytoin and other anticonvulsants reduce folate absorption, and trimethoprim interferes with folate metabolism (antifolate drugs).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.357 · Answer PDF p.372 · **Type:** Other (etiology) · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":8,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-009","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A 1-year-old child has folic acid deficiency since the age of 4 months, the best indicator of this deficiency is","o":["significant increment of mean corpuscular volume MCV","significant fall of reticulocytes count","high level of lactate dehydrogenase LDH","decrease level of RBC folate","increase number of hypersegmented neutrophils"],"k":3,"ex":"Red cell folate reflects tissue stores over the previous months and is the better measure of folate status; serum folate (below 4 ng/mL) varies with recent intake. In a child deficient since infancy, red cell folate is the most reliable indicator.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nMacrocytosis, hypersegmented neutrophils, reticulocytopenia and a high LDH are findings of megaloblastic anemia but they are late, indirect and not specific to folate (B12 deficiency gives the same picture).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.357 · Answer PDF p.372 (key \"9.(D).\" with no Nelson explanation) · **Type:** Investigation · **Depth:** Short","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":9,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-013","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"In hereditary spherocytosis, all the following will resolve postsplenectomy ExCEPT","o":["osmotic fragility","anemia","reticulocytosis","hyperbilirubinemia","hypoplastic/aplastic crises"],"k":0,"ex":"In hereditary spherocytosis the red cell membrane defect (spectrin, ankyrin, band 3) persists after splenectomy. Splenectomy removes the site of destruction, so hemolysis and its consequences (anemia, reticulocytosis, indirect hyperbilirubinemia, gallstone risk) settle, but the cells remain spherocytic and osmotic fragility only improves and is not corrected (Nelson).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nAnemia, reticulocytosis and hyperbilirubinemia are direct results of splenic destruction and resolve once the spleen is removed. Per Nelson's key, hypoplastic/aplastic crises also no longer occur after splenectomy. Splenectomy is delayed to about 6 years of age and requires vaccines and lifelong penicillin because of sepsis risk.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.358 · Answer PDF p.373 · **Type:** Pathophysiology · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":13,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-024","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A 9-month-old child with a hemoglobin concentration of 10 gm/dL and marked microcytosis; serum iron and total iron binding capacity are within normal limits; serum ferritin and hemoglobin electrophoresis are also normal. Of the following, the Most likely diagnosis is","o":["iron deficiency anemia","sideroblastic anemia","-thalassemia minor `[Greek letter lost in OCR]`","α-thalassemia trait","anemia due to chronic disease"],"k":3,"ex":"A mildly low hemoglobin with marked microcytosis and normal iron, TIBC and ferritin excludes iron deficiency and chronic disease. A normal hemoglobin electrophoresis (no raised HbA2) argues against β-thalassemia trait. α-Thalassemia trait gives a mild microcytic hypochromic anemia that is easily mistaken for iron deficiency, with normal electrophoresis; it is confirmed by DNA testing.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nIron deficiency has low iron and ferritin and high TIBC. Sideroblastic anemia has high or normal iron and high ferritin and is rare in this age. Anemia of chronic disease has low serum iron with normal or high ferritin. β-Thalassemia minor would show raised HbA2 on electrophoresis (about 5%).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.360 · Answer PDF p.374 · **Type:** Differential Diagnosis (clinical case) · **Depth:** Detailed","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":24,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-029","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Fragmentation hemolysis by mechanical injury may be seen in all the following EXCEPT","o":["extensive burns","Kasabach-Merritt syndrome","after cardiac surgery for prosthetic heart valve replacement","thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)","hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)"],"k":0,"ex":"Fragmentation (microangiopathic or mechanical) hemolysis results from red cells being sheared by fibrin strands, abnormal vessels or prosthetic material. Extensive burns instead damage red cells directly by heat, producing spherocytes rather than fragmented cells.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nKasabach–Merritt syndrome (giant hemangioma with consumption and shearing), TTP and HUS are classic microangiopathic hemolytic anemias with thrombocytopenia, and prosthetic valves cause mechanical fragmentation.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.361 · Answer PDF p.374 · **Type:** Pathophysiology · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":29,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-031","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Parvo virus B19 infection may cause transient pancytopenia in","o":["thalassemia major","sickle cell anemia","hereditary spherocytosis","hereditary elliptocytosis","pyruvate kinase deficiency"],"k":1,"ex":"Parvovirus B19 infects red cell precursors. In healthy people it causes mild illness, and classically isolated red cell aplasia, but in sickle cell disease (and immunodeficiency) the very short red cell survival makes the arrest of production dramatic and it can produce transient pancytopenia, the aplastic crisis.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nThalassemia major, hereditary spherocytosis, elliptocytosis and pyruvate kinase deficiency can also have parvovirus aplastic crises (the curriculum lists them for thalassemia and HS), but these are red cell aplasia with reticulocytopenia, not the pancytopenic picture keyed here.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF pp.361–362 · Answer PDF p.374 · **Type:** Complications · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":31,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-054","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A previously healthy 10-month-old boy develops pallor over 2 weeks. Examination is unremarkable except for pallor. Lab tests includes: hemoglobin, 4.9 g/dL; W8C, 6700/mm3; platelets count, 180000/mm3; MCV, 79 fL; reticulocyte count 0.8%; hemoglobin electrophoresis, normal. Of the following, the MosT likely cause for these findings is","o":["pure red cell aplasia","transient erythroblastopenia of childhood","thalassemia minor","aplastic anemia","parvovirus infection"],"k":1,"ex":"A previously healthy child aged 6 months to 5 years with an acute severe anemia limited to red cells, a normal MCV, low reticulocytes and normal white cells and platelets has transient erythroblastopenia of childhood (TEC), usually triggered by a viral infection, with spontaneous recovery over weeks.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nPure red cell aplasia is Diamond–Blackfan anemia, which presents earlier (usually 2–3 months) with a high MCV; this child has a normal MCV and a later, acute onset. Aplastic anemia involves all three lines (pancytopenia). Thalassemia minor gives a mild microcytic anemia and would not produce a hemoglobin of 4.9 g/dL; its electrophoresis also shows raised HbA2. Parvovirus-induced red cell aplasia occurs in children with underlying hemolytic anemia or immunodeficiency (Nelson).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.366 · Answer PDF p.376 · **Type:** Differential Diagnosis (clinical case) · **Depth:** Detailed","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":54,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-055","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"You are evaluating a 5-year-old male child with multiple ecchymoses distributed all over the extremities and trunk; examination reveal a palpable spleen 3 cm below the left costal margin. Lab findings include: hemoglobin, 11.3 g/dL; white blood cell count, 8700/mm3; platelets count, 21000/mm3. Of the following, the most proper NExT step is","o":["bleeding time","coagulation profile","platelets aggregation test","abdominal ultrasonography","bone marrow study"],"k":4,"ex":"Typical ITP has no organomegaly and no lymphadenopathy. A palpable spleen is an atypical feature, so a bone marrow examination is needed to exclude leukemia or aplastic anemia before labeling it ITP (the spleen is palpable in about 10% of ITP, but marrow is mandatory when the presentation is unusual).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nBleeding time is prolonged in ITP but no longer used and is unreliable. The coagulation profile (PT, PTT) is normal in ITP and would not explain thrombocytopenia. Platelet aggregation tests assess function when the count is normal. Abdominal ultrasound would confirm splenomegaly that is already evident and does not establish the cause.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.366 · Answer PDF p.376 · **Type:** Investigation · **Depth:** Detailed","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":55,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-058","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following is least likely to be associated with prolonged thrombocytopenia in children?","o":["immune thrombocytopenia","systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","HIV infection","type 2B von Willebrand disease","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS)"],"k":0,"ex":"About 70–80% of children with acute ITP recover spontaneously within 6 months (the curriculum: within 6–8 weeks), and only 10–20% develop chronic ITP, so ITP is the least likely of these to be prolonged.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nSLE is an important cause of persistent (chronic) thrombocytopenia, so SLE is screened for in chronic ITP. WAS is a congenital disorder with lifelong thrombocytopenia. HIV and type 2B VWD also cause persistent thrombocytopenia (Nelson).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.367 · Answer PDF p.376 · **Type:** Differential Diagnosis · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":58,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-060","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Microthrombocytes are MOsT likely to be seen in","o":["immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)","Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS)","Glanzmann's Thrombasthenia","type 2B von Willebrand disease"],"k":2,"ex":"Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome (X-linked recessive) has the triad of combined immunodeficiency, thrombocytopenia with small platelets, and eczema.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nIn ITP the platelets are normal or large. Bernard–Soulier syndrome has giant platelets (Nelson). Glanzmann thrombasthenia has a normal platelet count and size with abnormal function. Type 2B VWD does not produce microthrombocytes.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.367 · Answer PDF p.376 · **Type:** Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Short","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":60,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-063","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A 9-month-old boy appears pale. Examination reveals a palpable liver 3 cm below the right costal margin and palpable spleen 4 cm below the left costal margin. Lab findings include: hemoglobin concentration, 6.4 g/dl; mean corpuscular volume (MCV), 67 fL; and W8C, 15,000/mm3. Of the foilowing, The MosT valuable test to confirm the diagnosis is","o":["blood smear","serum iron, total iron binding capacity, and serum ferritin","hemoglobin electrophoresis","bone marrow study","abdominal ultrasonography"],"k":2,"ex":"Severe microcytic anemia in an infant with hepatosplenomegaly points to β-thalassemia major: severe anemia from 3–6 months, chronic hemolysis, and extramedullary hematopoiesis causing hepatosplenomegaly. Hemoglobin electrophoresis is diagnostic (HbF high, HbA low or absent, HbA2 raised).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nThe smear shows hypochromic microcytes with target cells but cannot reliably separate thalassemia from iron deficiency. Iron studies would be abnormal in iron deficiency (low iron and ferritin, high TIBC) but iron deficiency rarely causes marked hepatosplenomegaly (slight splenomegaly in 10–15%). Bone marrow study and abdominal ultrasound are not needed to make the diagnosis.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.368 · Answer PDF p.376 · **Type:** Investigation (clinical case) · **Depth:** Detailed","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":63,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-067","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Hematologic disease with upper limb orthopedic congenital abnormalities with presence of thumbs is MosT likely seen in","o":["Fanconi anemia","thrombocytopenia with Absent Radii (TAR) Syndrome","congenital Amegakaryocytic Thrombocytopenia","Glanzmann Thrombasthenia","Bernard-Soulier Syndrome"],"k":1,"ex":"TAR syndrome combines thrombocytopenia with absent radii but the thumbs are present, which distinguishes it from Fanconi anemia, where the thumbs are absent or abnormal.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nFanconi anemia has absent or abnormal thumbs and radii (with pancytopenia and other anomalies). Congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia, Glanzmann thrombasthenia and Bernard–Soulier syndrome are platelet disorders without limb malformations.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.369 · Answer PDF p.377 · **Type:** Differential Diagnosis · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":67,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-068","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following is not a feature of Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome (WAS)","o":["thrombocytopenia","eczema","recurrent otitis media and pneumonia","propensity to develop autoimmune disorders","giant platelets by blood smear"],"k":4,"ex":"WAS has thrombocytopenia with small platelets, eczema and immunodeficiency leading to recurrent infections (otitis media, pneumonia). Giant platelets are not a feature; the platelets are small.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nThrombocytopenia, eczema and recurrent infections are the classic triad. Autoimmune disease is also a recognized complication of WAS (Nelson).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.369 · Answer PDF p.377 (key \"68.(E).\" with no Nelson explanation) · **Type:** Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Short","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":68,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-069","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A S-year-old child has multiple bruises on her extremities and oral mucosal bleeding of 3 days duration; she had a mild respiratory tract infection 2 weeks before; physical examination was normal apart from multiple ecchymoses and petechiae. Of the following, the NExT diagnostic step is","o":["complete blood count","prothrombin time","bleeding time","partial thromboplastin time","antinuclear antibody titer"],"k":0,"ex":"This is the typical picture of acute ITP: a well child with petechiae, bruises and mucosal bleeding 1–3 weeks after a viral infection and no organomegaly. The diagnosis rests on clinical presentation plus a platelet count, so the CBC (with film) is the next step.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nPT and PTT are normal in ITP and are not needed first. Bleeding time is prolonged but outdated and unreliable. An ANA titer is considered later if ITP becomes chronic, to look for SLE.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.369 · Answer PDF p.377 (key \"69.(A).\" with no Nelson explanation) · **Type:** Investigation · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":69,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-072","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following children with acute immune thrombocytopenia is MOsT likely to have a benefit from splenectomy?","o":["an 8-year-old girl with multiple bruises and platelets count of 10,000/ml","a 6-year-old boy with mild epistaxis and platelets count of 20,000/ml","a 4-year-old boy with subdural hematoma and platelets count of 100,000/ml after receiving IVIG","an 11-year-old boy with severe rectal bleeding and platelets count of 200o0/ml not responding to medical treatment","a 13-year-old female adolescent with moderate menstrual bleeding and platelets count of 50000/ml"],"k":3,"ex":"Splenectomy is contraindicated in acute ITP except for severe life-threatening bleeding that fails medical therapy. The 11-year-old with severe rectal bleeding unresponsive to treatment fits this exception.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nMultiple bruises with a low count (A) or mild epistaxis (B) need observation or, if bleeding is significant, IVIG or steroids. The boy with a subdural hematoma (C) has already responded to IVIG with a platelet count of 100,000, so no splenectomy is needed. Moderate menstrual bleeding (E) is managed medically (hormonal therapy, steroids, IVIG).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.370 · Answer PDF p.377 · **Type:** Management · **Depth:** Detailed","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":72,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-073","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Of the following, the LEAsT likely feature of acute immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) is","o":["a preceding viral infection","isolated thrombocytopenia","mucocutaneous bleeding","isolated splenomegaly","increased number of megakaryocytes in the bone marrow"],"k":3,"ex":"Acute ITP usually follows a viral infection or vaccination, with isolated thrombocytopenia (normal Hb and WBC), petechiae, bruising, epistaxis and gum bleeding, and a marrow with increased megakaryocytes. There is no organomegaly or lymphadenopathy; splenomegaly occurs in only about 10% of cases, so isolated splenomegaly is the least likely feature.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nA preceding viral infection, isolated thrombocytopenia, mucocutaneous bleeding and increased marrow megakaryocytes are all typical.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.370 · Answer PDF p.377 · **Type:** Other (disease characteristics) · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":73,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-075","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"A 5-year-old child, with hemophilia A of severe type, presents to the emergency unit with a groin pain after a minor trauma to his back; his blood pressure is 60/30 mm Hg; his pulse rate is 180/min; he holds his right hip in a flexion position with internal rotation. Of the following, the NExT step in the management of this child is","o":["factor Vill replacement therapy","intravenous 1-deamino-8-d-arginine vasopressin (DDAVP)","factor VIll assay","abdominal ultrasonography","abdominal computed tomography (CT) scan"],"k":0,"ex":"A child with severe hemophilia A, hypotension, tachycardia and a hip held flexed after trauma has a major iliopsoas hemorrhage with hypovolemic shock, a life-threatening bleed. Immediate factor VIII replacement is the priority, before imaging or assays.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nDDAVP is only useful in mild hemophilia A (it releases endogenous factor VIII and VWF) and is ineffective in severe disease. A factor VIII assay would delay treatment of a known severe hemophiliac. Ultrasound or CT can confirm the hematoma but must not delay replacement.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.370 · Answer PDF p.377 · **Type:** Management (clinical case) · **Depth:** Detailed","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":75,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-078","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Inherited thrombocytopenias include all the following ExcEPT","o":["congenital Amegakaryocytic Thrombocytopenia","thrombocytopenia with Absent Radii (TAR) Syndrome","Glanzmann's Thrombasthenia","Bernard-Soulier Syndrome","Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome (WAS)"],"k":2,"ex":"Glanzmann thrombasthenia is a disorder of platelet function (defective aggregation) with a normal platelet count, so it is not a thrombocytopenia.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nTAR syndrome, Bernard–Soulier syndrome and Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome are listed among the congenital causes of decreased platelet production, and congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia is an inherited marrow failure with absent megakaryocytes (Nelson).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.371 · Answer PDF p.377 · **Type:** Other (classification) · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":78,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-079","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following is the best treatment for a 5-year-old girl with acute immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) and platelets count of 30,000/ml and mild petechial rash all over her body?","o":["intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG)","anti-D gamma globulin","corticosteroids","platelets transfusion","no treatment"],"k":4,"ex":"Acute ITP is benign and self-limiting in about 80% of children, usually resolving within 6–8 weeks. A child with only mild petechiae and a platelet count of 30,000 needs observation, avoidance of trauma and intramuscular injections, and home care.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nIVIG (2 g/kg, drug of choice), prednisolone and anti-D are reserved for severe or persistent bleeding or a very low count (the curriculum threshold is below 10,000; Nelson uses below 20,000). Platelet transfusion is avoided except in life-threatening bleeding.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.371 · Answer PDF p.377 · **Type:** Management · **Depth:** Moderate","nlSourceKey":"E","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":79,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-HEMATOLOGY-080","spec":"haematology","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The standard care for MosT children with severe hemophilia is","o":["prevention by F Vill replacement therapy to prevent spontaneous bleeding and early joint deformities","aggressive treatment by F Vill replacement therapy when significant bleeding occurs","avoid trauma","avoid aspirin and other NSAID","avoid violent contact sports"],"k":0,"ex":"Children with severe hemophilia (factor level below 1%) bleed spontaneously into joints and muscles. Regular prophylactic factor replacement (primary prophylaxis from age 2–3 years, every 2–3 days to keep the trough level above 2%) prevents spontaneous joint bleeding and the destructive arthropathy that follows, and is the standard of care.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\nOn-demand treatment of significant bleeds (B) is the older approach and leaves joints exposed to repeated bleeds. Avoiding trauma, aspirin/NSAIDs and contact sports (C, D, E) are important general measures but they do not prevent spontaneous bleeding in severe disease.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Question PDF p.371 · Answer PDF p.377 (key \"80.(A).\" with no Nelson explanation) · **Type:** Management · **Depth:** Short–Moderate","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Haematology","nlNumber":80,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-001","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Approximately 80% of blood cultures that will be positive are identified at","o":["1st 24 hr from incubation","25 - 48 hr from incubation","after 3 day from incubation","after 5 day from incubation","after 7 day from incubation"],"k":0,"ex":"About 80% of blood cultures that eventually turn positive are detected within the first 24 hours of incubation, and most of the rest by 48 hours.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Only a minority of positives (roughly the next 15–20%) appear between 25 and 48 hours.\n\n- **C.** Growth after 3 days is uncommon and usually reflects slow-growing or fastidious organisms.\n- **D.** Very few positives appear after 5 days (e.g., some fungi or fastidious bacteria).\n- **E.** Almost none; routine cultures are not held this long.\n\nNelson explanation:\n1.(A).\n\nHigh-yield:\nMost positive blood cultures flag within 24–48 h, so a culture negative at 48 h makes bacteremia unlikely.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 251 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":1,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-002","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Investigation","q":"The optimal amount of blood to collect from a pediatric patient for blood culture depend on","o":["age","weight","length","BMI","laboratory standards"],"k":1,"ex":"The volume of blood to be cultured is scaled to the child's body weight (circulating blood volume). A larger volume increases the chance of detecting low-grade bacteremia.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Age is only a rough surrogate; volume tables are based on weight.\n\n- **C.** Length is not used to set the volume.\n- **D.** BMI is not used.\n- **E.** Laboratory standards set bottle fill limits, but the clinical volume is individualised by weight.\n\nNelson explanation:\n2.(B).\n\nHigh-yield:\nBlood culture volume depends on weight (more blood = higher yield).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 251 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":2,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-003","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The IgM response occurs earlier in the illness, generally peaking at 7-10 days after infection, and usually disappears within a few weeks, but for some infections it can persist for months such as","o":["measles","mumps","rubella","hepatitis A","varicella"],"k":3,"ex":"IgM usually disappears within weeks, but in hepatitis A (and West Nile virus) it can persist for months. A high anti-HAV IgM therefore indicates recent infection.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Measles IgM falls within weeks.\n\n- **B.** Mumps IgM falls within weeks.\n- **C.** Rubella IgM is usually short-lived.\n- **E.** Varicella IgM is short-lived.\n\nNelson explanation:\n3.(D). Hepatitis A and West Nile Virus.\n\nHigh-yield:\nHepatitis A: IgM = recent infection, IgG = past infection/immunity.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 251 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":3,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-004","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Vaccines are defined as whole or parts of microorganisms administered to prevent an infectious disease. Which of the following is a live attenuated vaccine?","o":["hepatitis A","hepatitis B","pneumococcal","varicella","diphtheria"],"k":3,"ex":"Varicella vaccine is a live attenuated vaccine, as are MMR, BCG, oral polio, rotavirus, oral typhoid and intranasal influenza.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Hepatitis A vaccine is killed (inactivated).\n\n- **B.** Hepatitis B vaccine is a recombinant subunit vaccine.\n- **C.** Pneumococcal vaccine is a capsular polysaccharide (plain or conjugated) vaccine.\n- **E.** Diphtheria vaccine is a toxoid.\n\nNelson explanation:\n4.(D). Vaccines can consist of whole inactivated microorganisms (e.g-, polio and\nhepatitis A), parts of the organism (e.g., acellular pertussis, HPV, and hepatitis B),\npolysaccharide capsules (e.g., pneumococcal and meningococcal polysaccharide\nvaccines), polysaccharide capsules conjugated to protein carriers (e.g., Hib,\npneumococcal, and meningococcal conjugate vaccines), live attenuated\nmicroorganisms (measles, mumps, rubella, varicella, rotavirus, and live-attenuated\ninfluenza vaccines), and toxoids (tetanus and diphtheria)\n\nHigh-yield:\nLive vaccines: MMR, varicella, BCG, OPV, rota, yellow fever, intranasal flu, typhoid (oral) – contraindicated in immunodeficiency.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 251 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"D","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":4,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-005","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Which of the following is a T-lymphocyte independent vaccine?","o":["hepatitis A","hepatitis B 241 [Continuation on source page 252]","pneumococcal","varicella","diphtheria"],"k":2,"ex":"Plain polysaccharide vaccines (pneumococcal polysaccharide, meningococcal polysaccharide) are T-lymphocyte independent: poor response under 2 years, short-lived immunity and no booster effect.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Hepatitis A is an inactivated whole-virus vaccine (T-dependent).\n\n- **B.** Hepatitis B is a recombinant protein vaccine (T-dependent).\n- **D.** Varicella is a live vaccine (T-dependent).\n- **E.** Diphtheria is a toxoid (T-dependent).\n\nNelson explanation:\n5.(C). T-lymphocyte independent vaccines are associated with poor immune responses\nin children <2 yr of age, short-term immunity, and absence of an enhanced or booster\nresponse on repeat exposure to the antigen. With some polysaccharide vaccines,\nrepeat doses actually are associated with reduced responses, as measured by antibody\nconcentrations, compared to 1st doses (i.e., hyporesponsive). To overcome problems\nof plain polysaccharide vaccines, polysaccharides have been conjugated, or covalently\nlinked, to protein carriers, converting the vaccine to a T-lymphocyte dependent\nvaccine. In contrast to plain polysaccharide vaccines, conjugate vaccines induce higher-\navidity antibody, immunologic memory leading to booster responses on repeat\nexposure to the antigen, long-term immunity, and herd protection by decreasing\ncarriage of the organism.\n\nHigh-yield:\nConjugation to a carrier protein converts polysaccharide vaccines to T-dependent (e.g., PCV13, Hib).","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Clarification (key unchanged): the key applies to the plain polysaccharide form. PCV13, used in Libya, is conjugated and therefore T-dependent.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 251 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":5,"f":true},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-006","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Rotavirus vaccine should not be initiated for infants older than","o":["11 wk","13 wk","15 wk","17wk","19 wk"],"k":2,"ex":"The first dose of rotavirus vaccine must be given before 15 weeks of age (maximum 14 weeks 6 days); starting later is not recommended because of the intussusception risk.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Infants of 11 weeks can still start the vaccine.\n\n- **B.** Infants of 13 weeks can still start the vaccine.\n- **D.** Also beyond the limit, but the cut-off at which starting is no longer allowed is 15 weeks.\n- **E.** Also beyond the limit, but the cut-off at which starting is no longer allowed is 15 weeks.\n\nNelson explanation:\n6.(C).\n\nHigh-yield:\nRotavirus: start by 14 wk 6 d, finish by 8 months.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 252 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"C","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":6,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-007","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"The final dose of rotavirus vaccine must be administered no later than","o":["6 mo of age","8 mo of age","10 mo of age","12 mo of age","18 mo of age"],"k":1,"ex":"Whatever the schedule, the last rotavirus dose must be given by 8 months of age (Rotarix 2 doses, RotaTeq 3 doses).","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** 6 months is when the last RotaTeq dose is normally given, but the limit is later.\n\n- **C.** 10 months is beyond the allowed limit.\n- **D.** 12 months is beyond the allowed limit.\n- **E.** 18 months is beyond the allowed limit.\n\nNelson explanation:\n7.(B). Two rotavirus vaccines are available, RotaTeq (RV5) and Rotarix (RV1). With both\nvaccines, the 1st dose can be administered as early as 6 wk of age and must be\nadministered by 14 wk 6 days. The final dose in the series must be administered no\nlater than 8 mo of age. The Rv5 vaccine is administered in 3 doses at least 4 wk apart.\nThe RV1 vaccine is administered in 2 doses at least 4 wk apart. Immunization should\nnot be initiated for infants 15 wk of age and older as stated in the immunization\nschedule.\n\nHigh-yield:\nRotarix = 2 doses (2, 4 months); RotaTeq = 3 doses (2, 4, 6 months); last dose by 8 months.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 252 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":7,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-008","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Immunization is one of the most beneficial and cost-effective disease prevention measures. As a result of effective and safe vaccines, which of the following diseases has been eradicated?","o":["smallpox","polio","measles","rubella","pertussis"],"k":0,"ex":"Smallpox is the only one listed that has been eradicated (1980). Polio is close to eradication; the others still circulate.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **B.** Polio is near-eradicated but not eradicated.\n\n- **C.** Measles still causes outbreaks.\n- **D.** Rubella still occurs.\n- **E.** Pertussis is still endemic.\n\nNelson explanation:\n8.(A). As a result of effective and safe vaccines, smallpox has been eradicated; polio is\nclose to worldwide eradication.\n\nHigh-yield:\nSmallpox is the only human disease eradicated by vaccination.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":false,"nlReason":"","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 252 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"A","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":8,"f":false},{"id":"NEL-INFECTIOUS-009","spec":"infectious","topic":0,"tag":"Nelson Textbook","kind":"Knowledge","q":"Infants, children, and adolescents in Iraq are routinely immunized against","o":["10 diseases","11 diseases","12 diseases","13 diseases","14 diseases"],"k":1,"ex":"The source book (Iraqi edition) states that children there are routinely immunised against 11 diseases. This is a country-specific recall item.","more":"Why other options are incorrect:\n- **A.** Fewer than the source figure.\n\n- **C.** More than the source figure.\n- **D.** More than the source figure.\n- **E.** More than the source figure.\n\nNelson explanation:\n9.(B). Infants, children, and adolescents in the Iraq routinely are immunized against 11\ndiseases: BCG, poliomyelitis, hepatitis B, diphtheria, tetanus, pertussis, H. influenzae\ntype b (Hib) disease, rotavirus, measles, mumps and rubella.","nl":true,"nlClass":"Core Accepted","nlNeedsReview":true,"nlReason":"Answer Verification Required: the key reflects the Iraqi programme; the curriculum teaches the Libyan schedule, so the number is not curriculum knowledge. Source answer preserved.","nlSource":"Nelson MCQs (1).md · Infectious Diseases · question page 252 · answer page(s): see section manifest","nlSourceKey":"B","nlChapter":"Infectious Diseases & Vaccines","nlNumber":9,"f":true}]);